WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:16.292
With the check for nine hundred pounds in my pocket, I went to see Miss Skiffins' brother, who

2
00:00:16.332 --> 00:00:18.412
had arranged for Herbert's position at Clarriker's.

3
00:00:19.332 --> 00:00:23.994
He brought Clarriker to me, and I had the great satisfaction of completing the arrangements for Herbert's partnership.

4
00:00:24.894 --> 00:00:28.335
This was the only good thing I had done since I learned of my great expectations.

5
00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:34.341
Clarriker informed me that his shipping business was steadily improving, and with the money from me, he would

6
00:00:34.361 --> 00:00:37.281
now be able to establish a small branch office in Egypt.

7
00:00:38.142 --> 00:00:41.783
Herbert, in his new capacity as partner, would go out and take charge of it.

8
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:46.444
I felt as if my last anchor were loosening and I would soon be driven by the winds

9
00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:47.164
and the waves.

10
00:00:48.104 --> 00:00:52.625
But I had some consolation knowing that Herbert would soon come home and tell me this news himself,

11
00:00:53.066 --> 00:00:54.986
little suspecting that I had been involved in it.

12
00:00:56.406 --> 00:00:57.247
It was now March.

13
00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:01.708
My left arm was taking so long to heal that I was still unable to get a coat

14
00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:04.605
on. My right hand was tolerably restored.

15
00:01:05.586 --> 00:01:10.730
One Monday morning, while Herbert and I were at breakfast, I received a note from Wemmick: Burn this

16
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.710
as soon as you read it.

17
00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:15.934
On Wednesday you might do something if you feel able to try it.

18
00:01:16.854 --> 00:01:20.437
Herbert and I considered what to do because my injury was now a real issue.

19
00:01:20.457 --> 00:01:23.059
"I've thought it over," said Herbert.

20
00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:27.422
"Instead of taking a waterman to help us row, we should take Startop.

21
00:01:28.162 --> 00:01:30.304
He was my father's student and he likes us."

22
00:01:31.116 --> 00:01:33.197
"But how much would you tell him about what we're doing?"

23
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:34.657
"Very little.

24
00:01:35.477 --> 00:01:39.518
He doesn't need to know until Wednesday morning that there's an urgent reason for getting Provis away.

25
00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:41.699
Where will you go with Provis?"

26
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.700
It seemed to me that the place didn't matter as long as Provis was out of England.

27
00:01:47.581 --> 00:01:49.421
Any foreign steamer that would take us would do.

28
00:01:50.281 --> 00:01:54.782
Because foreign steamers left London at high tide, our plan was to get down the river earlier in

29
00:01:54.802 --> 00:01:57.023
the day and lie waiting in some quiet spot.

30
00:01:57.943 --> 00:02:00.104
But we needed to know when a steamer would pass by.

31
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.162
After breakfast we went out to pursue our investigations and found that a steamer bound for Hamburg, Germany,

32
00:02:06.202 --> 00:02:07.623
would likely suit our purposes.

33
00:02:08.624 --> 00:02:12.145
We also noted what other foreign steamers would leave London on the same tide.

34
00:02:13.086 --> 00:02:16.067
I then went to secure passports while Herbert went to see Startop.

35
00:02:16.927 --> 00:02:21.229
When we met again at one o'clock, I had the passports and Herbert had found Startop, who was

36
00:02:21.289 --> 00:02:22.010
willing to help us.

37
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:26.792
Herbert was going to Mill Pond Bank that evening to prepare Provis to meet us on Wednesday.

38
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.942
We would not communicate with Provis again until we took him on board my small boat.

39
00:02:32.822 --> 00:02:36.684
When I went home, I found a letter, which had been delivered by hand: If you are not

40
00:02:36.724 --> 00:02:39.966
afraid, come to the marshes tonight or tomorrow night at nine.

41
00:02:40.866 --> 00:02:45.088
Come to the little house by the kiln if you want information regarding your uncle Provis.

42
00:02:46.009 --> 00:02:46.809
Tell no one.

43
00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:48.730
You must come alone.

44
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:50.831
Bring this letter with you.

45
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:52.672
I didn't know what to do.

46
00:02:53.568 --> 00:02:57.550
The worst was that I had to decide quickly or I would miss the afternoon coach, which would

47
00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.050
take me down in time for tonight.

48
00:03:00.091 --> 00:03:02.732
Finally I decided to go because of the reference to Provis.

49
00:03:03.692 --> 00:03:07.314
I left Herbert a note, saying that I had gone to visit Miss Havisham to see how she

50
00:03:07.354 --> 00:03:09.815
was faring, and then I hurried off for the coach.

51
00:03:10.855 --> 00:03:12.176
It was dark before I arrived.

52
00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:17.498
Avoiding the Blue Boar, where I might be recognized by someone, I went to another inn.

53
00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:22.520
While my dinner was being prepared, I stopped briefly at Miss Havisham's and found that she was still

54
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.304
very ill, though somewhat better.

55
00:03:25.185 --> 00:03:28.726
Back at the inn, I was not able to cut my dinner because of my injured arm, so

56
00:03:28.746 --> 00:03:29.886
the landlord did it for me.

57
00:03:30.826 --> 00:03:36.988
Not recognizing me, he entertained me with my own story—the popular local version that Pumblechook was my benefactor.

58
00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:39.269
"Does this young man ever come back?"

59
00:03:39.649 --> 00:03:40.609
I asked about myself.

60
00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:44.490
"Yes, but he never visits Pumblechook, the man who made him."

61
00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.911
"And does Pumblechook complain about that?"

62
00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:49.491
"Oh yes," said the landlord.

63
00:03:49.511 --> 00:03:51.112
"All the time."

64
00:03:52.488 --> 00:03:57.153
I thought of long-suffering Joe and sweet-tempered Biddy, the ones who had the real right to complain about

65
00:03:57.213 --> 00:04:02.578
me. I had never felt so remorseful about my thanklessness to Joe as that night when I heard

66
00:04:02.618 --> 00:04:04.459
about the brazen impostor Pumblechook.

67
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.783
After dinner I put on my coat and looked for the letter, but could not find it.

68
00:04:09.744 --> 00:04:12.447
I had the uneasy feeling that I must have dropped it in the coach.

69
00:04:13.368 --> 00:04:17.632
It was a dark night, though the full moon rose as I went out upon the desolate marshes.

70
00:04:18.556 --> 00:04:22.680
I walked for another half hour before I approached the kiln and the little house.

71
00:04:23.741 --> 00:04:25.542
I saw a light in the house and went in.

72
00:04:26.403 --> 00:04:28.545
There was a candle on the table but no one was there.

73
00:04:29.446 --> 00:04:32.028
I stood just within the door, looking out into the night.

74
00:04:32.989 --> 00:04:36.212
Suddenly the candle went out and a rope was thrown over me from behind.

75
00:04:36.232 --> 00:04:38.053
"Now I've got you!"

76
00:04:38.454 --> 00:04:39.014
said a voice.

77
00:04:39.034 --> 00:04:40.996
"Who are you?"

78
00:04:41.336 --> 00:04:43.178
I cried, struggling as the man tied me up.

79
00:04:44.019 --> 00:04:45.140
"Help! Help!"

80
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.801
He struck a light and I was surprised to see Orlick.

81
00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.901
"Do you recognize this?"

82
00:04:51.842 --> 00:04:55.703
Orlick picked up a gun decorated with brass that I had seen before at Miss Havisham's.

83
00:04:56.623 --> 00:04:57.843
"You cost me that job.

84
00:04:58.283 --> 00:05:00.504
And you came between me and a young woman I liked."

85
00:05:01.504 --> 00:05:02.145
Oh, Biddy!

86
00:05:02.965 --> 00:05:04.005
"What are you going to do to me?"

87
00:05:04.385 --> 00:05:04.885
I asked him.

88
00:05:04.905 --> 00:05:06.946
"I'm going to kill you!"

89
00:05:07.806 --> 00:05:09.227
He leaned forward, staring at me.

90
00:05:09.247 --> 00:05:12.207
"You've been in my way ever since you were a child.

91
00:05:13.048 --> 00:05:14.208
And I'll tell you something else.

92
00:05:15.144 --> 00:05:17.546
It was me who struck down your sister and left her for dead.

93
00:05:18.346 --> 00:05:19.367
But that's your fault.

94
00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:22.489
You were always favored while I was bullied and beaten.

95
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.412
You did that, and now you're going to pay for it."

96
00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:29.395
I thought of crying out again, but I knew no one could hear me.

97
00:05:30.215 --> 00:05:32.717
My arms burned from the rope tied tightly around them.

98
00:05:32.737 --> 00:05:35.679
"And I'll tell you one more thing," said Orlick.

99
00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:38.521
"It was me you tripped over that night on your stairs.

100
00:05:39.462 --> 00:05:42.244
I've had a mind to kill you since you were down here for your sister's funeral.

101
00:05:43.184 --> 00:05:46.786
I've followed you everywhere and when I looked for you, I found your uncle Provis!"

102
00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:49.988
He held the candle close to my face and I turned away.

103
00:05:50.008 --> 00:05:52.429
"I know his name is Abel Magwitch."

104
00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:54.511
He grinned malevolently.

105
00:05:54.531 --> 00:05:56.332
"Beware Compeyson.

106
00:05:57.172 --> 00:05:58.353
Beware the gallows."

107
00:05:59.353 --> 00:06:02.255
He picked up a heavy hammer and I shouted out with all my might.

108
00:06:03.235 --> 00:06:06.557
I struggled and suddenly I heard voices and saw men attacking Orlick.

109
00:06:07.438 --> 00:06:11.020
He got free of them, leaped over the table, and flew out into the night.

110
00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:14.506
And then all went black and I knew no more.
